Z||000004||Standard||09-21-2001||07:38 PM||||you can turbo any engine you want, the question is "how long will it last?"<P>In the case of the duke.... not long  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">         <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shaun41178||09-21-2001||08:50 PM||||yes you can turbo the iron duke.  its relatively easy to do.  Just like the v6.  Get the turbo, run the plumbing and such.  There is plenty of room down in there to mount the turbo too.  Should be able to get an intercooler under there too from a Starion.  Or route the plumbing and mount up a FMIC from a Supra or something.  A stock duke can hold up to 15 psi no problem.  Might even be able to go higher with the intercooler.  The turbo will also increase the redline to give more power up top.  You can get around 200 hp with a turbo on a stock duke no problem.  I don't know why it isn't done more often honestly||||reg||6
Z||000006||Shaun41178||09-21-2001||08:52 PM||||Tabs there are 4 bangers in teh new z's as I am sure you know.  that is the one that was being talked about as he said 4 cyl.  I thin kit is a 2.4 liter in the z24 and is a DOHC that makes 150 hp.  I say just turbo the duke||||reg||1
Z||000007||ray b||09-21-2001||11:06 PM||||turbo duke=hand grenade<BR>go to the junk yard and get a 2.0 sunbird with turbo it bolts up to your trans. motor mounts?? but its been done was semi fact opt. in 88 back door deal.<BR>150 hp stock and it will live at 200 hp.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierospeeder||09-22-2001||12:11 AM||||Yes its possible.  Someplace on the forum, you have to do a search.  I might still have the jpeg of the magazine article of the manufacturer ,in the 80s, that had turbo kits for the 2.5l.   They are no longer in business, as i recall.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Posthumane||09-22-2001||12:54 AM||||Yes you can turbo any engine you want. Problem with the duke is its pretty weak on the botom end. You have to be careful with the amount of boost you run so you don't blow it into smitherines. <P>The engine that you mentioned is the quad 4. The new ones kinda suck at 150hp. If you can manage to find the 90-91 High Output version, like from a beretta WRZ, they are rated at 190hp from 2.3L (naturally aspirated), and are strong enuf to handle a turbo system w/out mass destruction of internal parts.||||reg||1
Z||000010||MinnGreenGT||09-22-2001||01:06 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B>...Just like the v6.  Get the turbo, run the plumbing and such.  There is plenty of room down in there to mount the turbo too.  Should be able to get an intercooler under there too from a Starion.  Or route the plumbing and mount up a FMIC from a Supra or something.  A stock duke can hold up to 15 psi no problem.  Might even be able to go higher with the intercooler.  The turbo will also increase the redline to give more power up top.  You can get around 200 hp with a turbo on a stock duke no problem.  I don't know why it isn't done more often honestly</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You must be kidding!  You make it sound like a simple relaible setup. There is a science to it still.  Yikes.  15psi without an intercooler would be like land mine with wheels and a Fiero badge.  200 HP with a turbo on a stock duke?  For like what- one or two 1/4 mile runs.  The Duke is NOT strong enough to last under those circumstances.  <P>The newer GM cars are designed more like imports.  Hell, they are imports  -the cavalier for example... strangely enough is sold in Japan as a "Toyota Cavalier."  same cavalier badge, same body, same engine, different mfg tag.  The technology  in those cars is drastically differnt and would requrie quite a bit of modification to run properly in your  Fiero.<P>My recommendation... do some research, read a good book on Turbo systems before going romping through the salvage yard looking for Turbo T-Birds and Starion intercoolers.  If you want two fast runs on your Duke before blowing it up- invest in a 125hp shot - nitrous system.  Realistically though, you could run a motorcycle 25hp shot in your duke without too much trouble!<P>[EDIT] If you want a turbo-4, stick with posthumane's idea and drop in a HO Quad-4... I've seen them N/A blow away many  V6s.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfpole.gif">   <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Leper||09-22-2001||02:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Simple Fiero:<BR><B>Yah i would like to know this also. Also does anybody know if a new sunfire or cav z24<BR>engine will fit I have always been curious about that. Would be nice to have a more powerful 4 cyl. Oh and if you wanted to be nuts then what about about the new RSX motor, mmm yummy! LOL</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The 2.4 in the Sunfire and Cav is close enough to a quad 4 (is not the same motor)that swapping one into a Fiero should be almost the same. You'd be on your own with the wiring though. The best part of the motor is the 150lb/ft of torque from 2400-4400rpms. They are easier to get than the 190 hp H/O quad4's too. And though they aren't as powerfull, can still be alot of fun. While there are turbo's and superchargers for the motor, there isn't really a good aftermarket for the engine.<P>Tabs, why won't a 3.1 fit in a Fiero?<P>I wouldn't recommend the turbo 2.0, but I'm biased since I had one blow up, almost in my face.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tabs||09-22-2001||08:20 AM||||I siad that a 3.1 will fit in a Fiero I've seen it done||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||09-22-2001||08:50 AM||||back when they were being made there was a turbo kit for the 4cyl. can't remember the name. if you have a local library with motor trend and/or road and track back issues to '84 there's an article about it. you're not going to find one now i'm sure, but you might get an idea or two. they were only good for about 140hp so investment in a different engine is probaly a better idea.  <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000014||MinnGreenGT||09-22-2001||09:59 AM||||I'm not sure which 3.1 you guys are referring to... the early 90's 3.1L used in Lumina Minivans and such- is the same motor as the Fiero's stock 2.8L.  Which I'm running a 3.1L from a Lumina Minivan.  If you're referring to a newer version of the motor (commonly referred to as a 3100) I've seen that in two Fieros many years ago.  The install appeared stock, except the writing on the intake plenum was facing the wrong way...||||reg||1
Z||000015||Standard||09-22-2001||04:55 PM||||The problem with the quad 4's is the pesky weak head gasket.    And after 96 they're called the 2.4 Twin Cam.  <P>Quad 4 HO's also have 10:1 compression stock.. not the best for a turbo motor.  And they are rated at 180 HP stock.  The quad 4 that's rated at 190 is the w-41, using different cam's and a different chip.<P>I'm still learning all the differences, but from what I've gathered the 90-92 HO's have the best flowing head's, intake and exhaust manifolds, although they're all rated at 180hp sfaik.  Virtually all quads were choked on the exhaust side from the factory, and since you need custom exhaust to put one in a Fiero anyways, go with at least 2.25".||||reg||1
Z||000016||Shaun41178||09-22-2001||06:52 PM||||yes turboing a duke is really simple.  Anyone can do it.  IRM made the kit and sold it back in the day but not anymore.  Dont' worry though.  Its really simple.  In fact I am working on a kit for one right now.  Thats right a kit.  Projected cost will be in the $1200 dollar range for you cheapos with hp being around 160-180 reliable carefree hp on  a stock bottom end.  Will include all necessary equipment.  No other mods will be necessary.  <P>My turn to jump on the turbo bandwagon now but I am going with the best engine that was put in a Fiero to start with.  Good ole' Iron Duck.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com||||reg||14
